MonkMode
========

MonkMode is a tamper-resistant website/app self-control blocker for Windows.
It is a personal fork of the open-source Cold Turkey blocker (GPLv3), rebranded
and being hardened so that a block, once started, cannot be casually removed
before its timer expires.

Project layout
--------------
  /MonkMode       <-- Main GUI application (writes the hosts file, installs the service)
  /MonkMode_srv   <-- LocalSystem Windows service "MONKMODE" (locks the hosts file,
                       kills blocked processes, lifts the block when time is up)
  /MM_notify      <-- User-level background process: triggers the popup when time is up
  /MM_notify2     <-- Secondary user-level watcher
  /MM_popup       <-- The "time's up" popup shown to the user

See ARCHITECTURE.md for the full component map and the current bypass surface.

Building
--------
- Target framework: .NET 8 (net8.0-windows), VB.NET, SDK-style projects.
- Open MonkMode.sln (repo root) in Visual Studio 2022, or build from the CLI:
      dotnet build MonkMode.sln -c Release
  Output exes land in each project's bin/Release/net8.0-windows/.
- Requires the .NET 8 SDK. The GUI (MonkMode.exe) requests Administrator
  elevation (it writes the hosts file and installs the service).
- Starting a block installs and starts the Windows service "MONKMODE" via the
  Service Control Manager (see MonkMode/ServiceTools.vb).

Migration note
--------------
Originally VB.NET 2010 / .NET Framework 2.0. Migrated to .NET 8:
- All five projects converted to SDK-style project files.
- Authored MonkMode/ServiceTools.vb (advapi32 P/Invoke) to replace the
  third-party "ServiceTools" helper the original referenced but never shipped.
- Dropped the unused Microsoft.VisualBasic.PowerPacks reference and the dead
  installutil-based service installer (Class1.vb); the GUI installs the service
  programmatically.

Removing the service (for development only)
-------------------------------------------
  Run as Administrator:  sc delete MONKMODE

  NOTE: this removal path is exactly the kind of bypass MonkMode's hardening
  work (Phase 3) is meant to close. It is documented here only because it is
  needed while developing.

